# name: (story, controversy, change)
D={
"Printing press":("Johannes Gutenberg, a goldsmith from Mainz, spent years in secret developing a system of movable metal type in the 1440s: he invented a hand mould that could cast thousands of identical letters from a lead-tin-antimony alloy, an oil-based ink that stuck to metal, and adapted a screw press used for wine and paper. To finance it he borrowed heavily from the merchant Johann Fust. Around 1455 he printed about 180 copies of the 42-line Bible.","Gutenberg never got rich: in 1455 Fust sued him for repayment, won, and took over the workshop and much of the equipment, printing with Gutenberg's former assistant Peter Schöffer. Gutenberg's name appears on none of his books. Korea had already printed with metal type (Jikji, 1377) and China with movable clay type (Bi Sheng, about 1040), but European alphabets suited movable type far better than thousands of Chinese characters.","Within 50 years, presses in over 250 European cities had produced perhaps 20 million books. Printing spread Renaissance learning and the Scientific Revolution, made Luther's Reformation possible (his pamphlets sold hundreds of thousands), standardised languages and spelling, and gave rise to newspapers. The Catholic Church responded with an Index of Forbidden Books."),
"Telescope":("In 1608 the Dutch spectacle-maker Hans Lipperhey applied for a patent on a device of two lenses in a tube that made distant objects appear closer; two other Dutchmen, Jacob Metius and Zacharias Janssen, claimed it too, so no patent was granted. In 1609 Galileo Galilei heard of it, built his own with 20× magnification and turned it to the sky, discovering Jupiter's four largest moons, Venus's phases, sunspots and craters on the Moon. Kepler and Newton later improved designs.","Galileo's discoveries supported Copernicus's Sun-centred model, bringing him into conflict with the Church; in 1633 the Inquisition forced him to recant and placed him under house arrest. The Church formally admitted its error only in 1992.","The telescope transformed humanity's place in the universe, showing that Earth is not unique. It enabled navigation, astronomy and, much later, telescopes like Hubble and James Webb."),
"Steam engine":("In 1712 Thomas Newcomen, an English ironmonger, built an atmospheric steam engine to pump water from flooded mines; it was very inefficient. James Watt, an instrument maker at Glasgow University repairing a model Newcomen engine in 1763–65, realised that cooling the cylinder each stroke wasted fuel, and added a separate condenser. With Birmingham industrialist Matthew Boulton, he built engines from 1776 and added rotary motion, so steam could drive factory machinery. Richard Trevithick and George Stephenson later built high-pressure engines for locomotives.","Watt aggressively defended his patents, which some historians argue slowed the adoption of better high-pressure engines until his patent expired in 1800. The steam engine relied on coal, and cotton for British mills came from American plantations worked by enslaved people and from India.","Steam power drove the Industrial Revolution: factories, railways and steamships, urbanisation, and a huge rise in coal burning that began human-made climate change. It allowed Britain to dominate world trade and conquer colonies, and Indian handloom textiles were undercut by machine-made cloth."),
"Vaccination":("Variolation — deliberately infecting people with smallpox material to cause a mild case — was practised in India, China and the Ottoman Empire for centuries; Lady Mary Wortley Montagu introduced it to England in 1721. In 1796 Edward Jenner, an English country doctor, tested the milkmaids' belief that cowpox protected against smallpox: he inoculated eight-year-old James Phipps with cowpox from milkmaid Sarah Nelmes, then exposed him to smallpox, and the boy did not fall ill. He called it vaccination, from Latin vacca (cow).","Jenner's experiment on a child without modern consent would be unethical today. Anti-vaccination leagues formed in the 19th century, and cartoons showed people sprouting cow heads. In India, colonial vaccination drives sometimes met resistance because the cowpox lymph came from calves and the British ignored local variolators (tikadars).","Vaccination led to the eradication of smallpox, declared in 1980 after the last natural case in Somalia (1977); India's last case was in 1975. Vaccines against polio, measles and COVID-19 followed."),
"Railway":("George Stephenson, a self-taught colliery engineer, built the Locomotion No. 1 for the Stockton and Darlington Railway (1825), the first public railway to use steam locomotives. His son Robert's Rocket won the Rainhill Trials in 1829, and the Liverpool and Manchester Railway (1830) became the first inter-city line. Railways spread rapidly; India's first passenger train ran from Bombay to Thane on 16 April 1853.","At the opening of the Liverpool and Manchester Railway, MP William Huskisson was killed by the Rocket — the first widely reported railway death. In India, railways were built with British capital guaranteed a 5% return paid by Indian taxpayers, routes were designed to move raw materials to ports and troops to trouble spots, and third-class Indian passengers faced segregation.","Railways shrank distances, created standard time zones, enabled mass travel and national markets, and moved famine grain — but also exported it during famines. In India they later helped unite the national movement."),
"Telegraph":("In 1837 William Cooke and Charles Wheatstone patented an electric telegraph in Britain, and Samuel Morse, an American painter who had started working on it after his wife died before he could reach her, developed his own with a code of dots and dashes. Morse sent 'What hath God wrought' from Washington to Baltimore in 1844. The first transatlantic cable was laid in 1858, and India was linked to Britain in 1870. William O'Shaughnessy built India's first lines in the 1850s.","During the 1857 rebellion, the telegraph allowed British officers to coordinate; one rebel reportedly called it 'the accursed string that strangled us'. Morse's code was also partly developed by his assistant Alfred Vail, who received little credit.","For the first time, information travelled faster than people, enabling news agencies like Reuters, global financial markets and imperial control."),
"Photography":("Joseph Nicéphore Niépce made the first surviving photograph, 'View from the Window at Le Gras', in 1826 or 1827, needing hours of exposure. His partner Louis Daguerre developed the daguerreotype (1839), and in Britain William Henry Fox Talbot invented the negative-positive process. The French government bought Daguerre's patent and gave it 'free to the world'.","Photography was quickly used for colonial anthropology, classifying Indian 'castes and tribes' in works like The People of India (1868–75), and for documenting famines and wars. Photos could also be faked: Lincoln's head was placed on another man's body in a famous portrait.","Photography changed memory, journalism, art and science, and led to cinema."),
"Telephone":("Alexander Graham Bell, a Scottish-born teacher of the deaf in Boston, worked on transmitting speech electrically with his assistant Thomas Watson. On 14 February 1876, his lawyer filed a patent application hours before Elisha Gray filed a caveat for a similar device; Bell's patent was granted on 7 March, and on 10 March he spoke the first words: 'Mr Watson, come here, I want to see you.'","The invention is one of history's great disputes. Italian immigrant Antonio Meucci had demonstrated a voice-communication device ('telettrofono') in the 1850s and filed a caveat in 1871 but could not afford to renew it; in 2002 the US House of Representatives passed a resolution honouring Meucci's work. Gray's supporters alleged a patent examiner showed Bell details of Gray's liquid transmitter, which Bell later used. Bell's company won around 600 lawsuits.","The telephone created a global communication network and companies like AT&T, and led eventually to mobile phones and the internet."),
"Light bulb":("Many inventors made electric lights before Edison, including Humphry Davy's arc lamp (1800s) and Joseph Swan's carbon-filament bulb in England (demonstrated 1878–79). Thomas Edison's team at Menlo Park tested thousands of materials and in October 1879 produced a carbonised-filament bulb that lasted over 13 hours, then a bamboo filament lasting over 1,000 hours. Edison's real achievement was a whole system: generators, wiring, meters and the Pearl Street power station (1882).","Swan sued Edison in Britain, and they formed a joint company, Ediswan. Edison is often credited with inventions largely developed by his employees. In the 'war of the currents', Edison promoted direct current and his ally Harold Brown publicly electrocuted dogs and horses with alternating current to show it was dangerous; Edison's company supported the development of the electric chair with AC. Alternating current, developed by Tesla and Westinghouse, won.","Electric light extended the working day, transformed cities and homes, and created the electricity industry."),
"Radio":("James Clerk Maxwell predicted electromagnetic waves in 1865, and Heinrich Hertz demonstrated them in 1887. In 1895 Jagadish Chandra Bose in Calcutta demonstrated microwave transmission, ringing a bell at a distance, while Guglielmo Marconi in Italy and Britain developed practical wireless telegraphy and sent signals across the Atlantic in 1901. Reginald Fessenden broadcast speech and music in 1906.","Marconi's 1901 transatlantic receiver used a mercury coherer that some historians say was based on Bose's design, which Bose had chosen not to patent. In 1943 the US Supreme Court invalidated key Marconi patents, recognising prior work by Nikola Tesla, Oliver Lodge and John Stone Stone. Marconi shared the 1909 Nobel Prize with Karl Ferdinand Braun.","Radio saved lives at sea (the Titanic's distress calls), enabled broadcasting, propaganda (Hitler's speeches, Bose's Azad Hind Radio) and later television and mobile phones."),
"Aeroplane":("Orville and Wilbur Wright, bicycle makers from Dayton, Ohio, studied birds and gliders and built their own wind tunnel to test 200 wing shapes. Their key innovation was 'wing-warping' for control around three axes. On 17 December 1903 at Kill Devil Hills near Kitty Hawk, Orville made the first controlled, sustained powered flight of 12 seconds and 37 m; Wilbur flew 260 m later that day.","Several others claimed earlier flights. Gustave Whitehead reportedly flew in Connecticut in 1901, a claim most historians reject for lack of evidence. Brazil celebrates Alberto Santos-Dumont, who flew publicly in Paris in 1906, as the true inventor, since the Wrights' early flights had few witnesses and used a launch rail. The Smithsonian long credited Samuel Langley's failed aerodrome, leading to a feud until 1942; its contract to display the Wright Flyer requires crediting the Wrights.","Within 11 years aircraft were used in World War I, and air power later enabled the bombing of cities, mass tourism and global trade."),
"Antibiotics":("In September 1928 Alexander Fleming returned from holiday to his London lab and noticed that a mould, Penicillium notatum, had killed bacteria in a culture dish. He published, but could not purify it. In 1939–41 Howard Florey, Ernst Chain and Norman Heatley at Oxford purified penicillin and tested it; the first patient, policeman Albert Alexander, improved but died when supplies ran out. American companies mass-produced it for D-Day in 1944.","Fleming became famous, but Florey and Chain did much of the work; all three shared the 1945 Nobel Prize. Fleming warned in his Nobel lecture that misuse would breed resistant bacteria — today antimicrobial resistance kills over a million people a year.","Antibiotics turned once-deadly infections into treatable conditions and made modern surgery safer."),
"Computer":("Charles Babbage designed mechanical computing engines in the 1830s, and Ada Lovelace wrote what is considered the first computer program. Alan Turing described a universal computing machine in 1936. During World War II, Tommy Flowers built Colossus (1944) to break German codes at Bletchley Park, and in the US, John Mauchly and J. Presper Eckert built ENIAC (1945), programmed by six women including Kathleen McNulty and Jean Bartik.","Colossus was kept secret until the 1970s, so the US was long credited with the first electronic computer. The women who programmed ENIAC were left out of photos and histories for decades.","Computers transformed science, business and daily life, leading to personal computers, the internet and AI."),
"Nuclear energy":("In December 1938 Otto Hahn and Fritz Strassmann in Berlin discovered nuclear fission, explained by Lise Meitner and Otto Frisch. On 2 December 1942, under the stands of a squash court at the University of Chicago, Enrico Fermi's team achieved the first self-sustaining nuclear chain reaction in Chicago Pile-1. The first nuclear power plant to supply a grid opened at Obninsk, USSR, in 1954; India's first reactor, Apsara, started in 1956 under Homi Bhabha.","Lise Meitner, a Jewish physicist who fled Nazi Germany, was excluded from Hahn's 1944 Nobel Prize — now widely seen as an injustice. Nuclear disasters at Chernobyl (1986) and Fukushima (2011) raised fears.","Nuclear energy offers low-carbon electricity but raises issues of waste and weapons."),
"Internet":("In 1969 the US Defense Department's ARPANET connected four universities; Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn designed the TCP/IP protocols (1974) that let different networks communicate, adopted on 1 January 1983. Tim Berners-Lee invented the World Wide Web at CERN in 1989–91. India's public internet launched via VSNL on 15 August 1995.","A popular myth says ARPANET was built to survive nuclear war; its creators say it was to share computing resources. Later revelations by Edward Snowden (2013) showed governments conducting mass surveillance of internet traffic.","The internet transformed communication, commerce, politics and knowledge."),
"Smartphone":("IBM's Simon (1994) was an early smartphone. Apple's iPhone, unveiled by Steve Jobs in January 2007, combined a touchscreen, internet and apps, and Google's Android (2008) spread smartphones globally, including cheap Indian phones.","Smartphone minerals like cobalt are mined in Congo, sometimes by children, and phones are assembled in factories criticised for harsh conditions.","Smartphones put the internet, cameras, maps and payments into billions of pockets."),
"Automobile":("Karl Benz patented the first practical petrol-powered car, the Motorwagen, in 1886; his wife Bertha drove it 106 km in 1888 to prove it worked. Henry Ford's moving assembly line (1913) made the Model T affordable.","General Motors and others pushed leaded petrol from 1923, invented by Thomas Midgley, despite workers dying from lead poisoning; lead pollution harmed children's brains worldwide until it was phased out (finally in 2021).","Cars transformed cities, suburbs and oil politics."),
"Gunpowder":("Chinese Taoist alchemists seeking an elixir of immortality mixed saltpetre, sulphur and charcoal, producing gunpowder by the 9th century. The Song used it in fire lances, bombs and rockets. It spread via the Mongols to the Islamic world and Europe.","Ironically, a search for eternal life produced one of history's deadliest inventions.","Gunpowder ended castle warfare and led to guns and cannon that helped empires such as the Ottomans and Mughals."),
"Paper":("Cai Lun, a eunuch official of the Han court, presented an improved method in 105 CE, using tree bark, hemp, rags and fishing nets; older paper fragments show earlier origins.","Paper's spread to the Islamic world after 751 remains linked to the legend of Chinese prisoners at Talas.","Paper made books, records and money cheap."),
"Zero and decimal place value":("Indian mathematicians developed a place-value decimal system; Brahmagupta (628) first gave rules for zero as a number. The Bakhshali manuscript shows a dot for zero. Al-Khwarizmi spread these numerals, and Fibonacci introduced them to Europe in 1202.","Europeans called them 'Arabic numerals', obscuring their Indian origin.","Indian numerals underpin all modern mathematics and computing."),
}
